    <section>
      <p class="wide">
        Configure Simple Java Mail through its <strong>Java API</strong>, <strong>system properties</strong>, <strong>environment variables</strong>, or
        <strong>property files</strong>. The <a href="/spring.html">Spring integration</a> reads the same settings from Spring's environment.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="introduction">
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        The Java API and configuration files work together. A configuration snapshot resolves its sources in the order you list them, with later
        non-blank values winning. Calls on a builder then win over ordinary snapshot values copied into that builder.
        <a href="#section-programmatic-api-common" id="section-programmatic-api-common" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Programmatic API - common settings</h2>

  <section>
    <div>
      <p>
        Start with one <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> instance. Ask it for a fresh builder whenever you start an independent
        email or Mailer construction flow. The public builder interfaces contain the complete option reference.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p>
        Without a configured transport strategy, the Mailer uses <code class="inline language-java">SMTP</code> with opportunistic TLS,
        even when you supply a username and password. It tries STARTTLS when advertised and may continue in plaintext when it is absent;
        a failed TLS upgrade stops the connection. Choose <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code> or
        <code class="inline language-java">SMTPS</code> if encryption is required. See the
        <a href="security.html#section-authentication-methods">transport strategy comparison</a> for the differences.
        Extra properties that disable mandatory STARTTLS are <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#mandatory-starttls">rejected when building the Mailer</a>.
      </p>
<pre><code class="small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

// start with a fresh builder
MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; mailerBuilder = mail.mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServer("smtp.host.com", 25, "username", "password");
// or
mailerBuilder = mail.mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServerHost("smtp.host.com")
  .withSMTPServerPort(25)
  .withSMTPServerUsername("username")
  .withSMTPServerPassword("password");
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="small">
// omit credentials for an anonymous server only if the factory has none configured
// a shorter overload preserves any credentials already on the builder
mail.mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.host.com", 25);
// or
mail.mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServerHost("smtp.host.com")
  .withSMTPServerPort(25);

// clear only these credentials without changing the host or port
mailerBuilder.clearSMTPServerUsername().clearSMTPServerPassword();

// require STARTTLS, also when authenticating with username/password
mailerBuilder.withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS);

// separate choice: explicitly skip STARTTLS for a plain SMTP connection
mailerBuilder.withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP)
  .withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
      .withOpportunisticTLS(false)
      .build());

// anonymous SOCKS proxy
mailerBuilder.withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
    .withHost("proxy.host.com")
    .withPort(1080)
    .build());

// or authenticated proxy
mailerBuilder
  .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
      .withHost("proxy.host.com")
      .withPort(1080)
      .withUsername("proxy username")
      .withPassword("proxy password")
      .build());
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="small">
// anonymous smtp + anonymous proxy + default SMTP protocol strategy
mailerBuilder
        .withSMTPServerHost("smtp.host.com").withSMTPServerPort(25)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("proxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .build());
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="small">
// configure everything!
mail.mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.host.com", 587, "user@host.com", "password")
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("socksproxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .withUsername("proxy user")
            .withPassword("proxy password")
            .build())
        .buildMailer()
        .sync().sendMail(email);
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="small">
// preconfigured Session?
mail.mailerBuilder(session);

// preconfigured but you need anonymous proxy?
mail.mailerBuilder(session)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("socksproxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .build());

// preconfigured but you need authenticated proxy?
mail.mailerBuilder(session)
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("socksproxy.host.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .withUsername("proxy user")
            .withPassword("proxy password")
            .build());
</code></pre>
      <p>Use one Mailer per Session. Another Mailer cannot use that Session, or a second Session sharing its
        <code class="inline language-java">Properties</code>, until the first Mailer has shut down successfully. Reuse the existing Mailer for
        concurrent sending, or create independent Sessions with independent Properties copied from your original SMTP settings.
        Use <code class="inline language-java">Session.getInstance(...)</code> rather than
        <code class="inline language-java">Session.getDefaultInstance(...)</code> when you need separate Sessions.</p>
      <p>A supplied Session keeps its connection settings unless you explicitly replace them through the builder, additional Jakarta Mail
        properties or mandatory locks. Ordinary factory connection defaults do not replace its timeouts, local binding, EHLO name or
        certificate trust. This is independent of whether the Session previously belonged to another Mailer.</p>
      <p>If a setting comes from an inherited <code>Properties</code> default, clearing it cannot remove that default. Such a clear is rejected
        before changing the Session. Supply a replacement value or create a new Session with independent Properties containing only the
        settings you want.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-programmatic-api-other" id="section-programmatic-api-other" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Programmatic API - other settings</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        The mailer builder also covers validation, diagnostics, timeouts, local binding, shared signing defaults, and
        <a href="features.html#section-extension-points">extension points</a>.
      </p>
      <p>Keep SMTP setup direct and collect advanced settings into immutable values. Build a value without opening connections or starting workers,
        then supply it through the corresponding Mailer method:</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Mailer method</th><th>Configuration value</th><th>Settings</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>withConnectionPoolConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionPoolConfig</code></td><td>Connection counts, claim timeout, expiration and clustering.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withAsyncExecutionConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>AsyncExecutionConfig</code></td><td>Worker count, keep-alive, waiting queue and admission, or a supplied executor.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withProxyConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>SocksProxyConfig</code></td><td>SOCKS endpoint, credentials and local bridge port.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withSendingLimitsConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>SendingLimitsConfig</code></td><td>Message/recipient rates and shared allowance.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withConnectionConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionConfig</code></td><td>Socket timeouts, local binding, EHLO hostname and legacy content compatibility.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withTlsConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>TlsConfig</code></td><td>Trust, hostname checks, SSL factories and optional STARTTLS.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withValidationConfig(...)</code></td><td><code>ValidationConfig</code></td><td>Address policy, warning behavior and maximum prepared email size.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>Each setter replaces that group's supplied choices. Omitted fields inherit the factory snapshot rather than keeping the previous group's values.
        Use <code>mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()</code>, for example, to retain choices while editing a group, or
        <code>useConfiguredConnectionPoolConfig()</code> to restore inheritance for the whole group. Group builders own detailed inspection and
        clear/reset methods; root getters inspect supplied groups. Effective settings remain available on the built Mailer.</p>
      <p>An explicit empty Java string remains a supplied choice and follows that setting's validation or clearing rules.
        It does not restore a captured value. This differs from blank entries in property sources, which are treated as absent.
        Use the named clear or <code>useConfigured...</code> operation to express either intent explicitly.</p>
      <p><code>ConnectionConfig.Builder.withSessionTimeout(...)</code> sets connect, read and write timeouts together.
        Use <code>withConnectTimeout(...)</code>, <code>withReadTimeout(...)</code> or <code>withWriteTimeout(...)</code> to adjust one operation.
        The last setter affecting an operation wins; see the <a href="#socket-timeouts">timeout examples and precedence</a>.</p>
      <p>The group's getters inspect supplied choices, including unset values. On the built Mailer,
        <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getConnectTimeout()</code>, <code>getReadTimeout()</code> and <code>getWriteTimeout()</code>
        report effective construction settings. <code>getSessionTimeout()</code> returns their common <code>Duration</code> only when all three
        are known and equal, otherwise <code>null</code>. Null does not mean unlimited, and inspection does not guess provider defaults or track
        direct Session mutations after construction. The group's common getter follows the same equality rule for its supplied choices.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="wide">
      <h4 id="configuration-operations">Setting, clearing and restoring a choice</h4>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Meaning at the documented scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>withX(value)</code></td><td>Supply a choice. Group setters replace the supplied group; collection methods can append where their contract says so.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>clearX()</code></td><td>Remove that scope's choice. Another fallback can apply; some clears explicitly remove a captured setting too.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>resetX()</code></td><td>Select the published built-in fallback, including absence when that is the fallback.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>useConfiguredX()</code></td><td>Restore captured configuration for that member or group.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Builder group getters</td><td>Inspect supplied choices, including absence and explicit-clear flags, without resolving or validating runtime settings or allocating resources. This includes <code>getMailSendRetryPolicy()</code>; use the built Mailer's <code>OperationalConfig</code> for resolved retry settings.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Built Mailer configuration getters</td><td>Inspect the effective construction settings. They do not track later direct Session mutations.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>Not every setting has every operation. The setter's contract defines its scope, and configuration locks still apply.
        For example, <code>resetMaxSize()</code> chooses the library pool maximum, while <code>useConfiguredMaxSize()</code> restores the captured maximum.
        Neither changes the other supplied members.</p>
      <p>Suppose the factory captured <code>simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.maxsize=7</code>. Replacing the pool group discards its previous
        supplied maximum and claim timeout:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-java">ConnectionPoolConfig firstPoolChoices = ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
    .withMaxSize(9)
    .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(firstPoolChoices);

mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
    .withCoreSize(1)
    .build());
// Supplied max size and claim timeout are now null.
// The built Mailer resolves max size to the captured 7.</code></pre>
      <p>To make the same core-size change while retaining the original supplied maximum and claim timeout, edit that value instead:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(firstPoolChoices.toBuilder()
    .withCoreSize(1)
    .build());
// Supplied max size remains 9 and claim timeout remains two seconds.</code></pre>
      <p>Use <code>mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()</code> when editing the builder's current group.
        These pool-group edits leave previously built values and Mailers unchanged. For a caller-owned Session, removing an explicit connection choice restores that
        Session's baseline where applicable; it does not start applying ordinary factory connection defaults.</p>
      <p>Clearing creation-age expiration explicitly disables even a captured age setting. Restoring inheritance is a separate operation;
        both retain the group's other choices, including idle expiration:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-java">ConnectionPoolConfig noCreationAge = mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()
    .clearExpireAfterCreation()
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(noCreationAge);
// getExpireAfterCreation() is null; isExpireAfterCreationCleared() is true.

ConnectionPoolConfig inheritedCreationAge = noCreationAge.toBuilder()
    .useConfiguredExpireAfterCreation()
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(inheritedCreationAge);
// getExpireAfterCreation() is still null; isExpireAfterCreationCleared() is false.
// The built Mailer can use the factory's captured age setting again.</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <pre><code class="small">// report missing fields, invalid addresses and CRLF suspicions as warnings,
// then continue with SMTP handling
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder()
    .withClientValidationWarningsOnly(true)
    .build());
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="small">// apply your own local email-size limit, independently of the SMTP server
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder()
    .withMaximumEmailSize(10_000_000)
    .build());</code></pre>
      <p>Managed Angus sends also check the prepared content against the connected server's advertised SIZE limit automatically.
        There is no additional property or builder setting. This connected check is separate from the local limit above and offline rehearsal;
        see <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-message-size">message size diagnostics</a>.</p>
		<pre><code class="small">// make the underlying Jakarta Mail Session produce debug output
mailerBuilder
    .withDebugConfig(SessionDebugConfig.builder()
        .withEnabled(true)
        .withOutput(SessionDebugOutput.SLF4J) // STDOUT, STDERR or SLF4J
        .build());
</code></pre>
      <p><code>SessionDebugConfig</code> keeps the enabled flag and output selection together. On its builder, <code>withOutput(...)</code>
        and <code>withPrinter(...)</code> select one destination. <code>useConfiguredOutput()</code> restores the factory's captured output
        selection, leaving a supplied Session's output unchanged when no output was configured. Borrowed streams are never closed.</p>
      <p><code>getEnabled()</code>, <code>getOutput()</code> and <code>getPrinter()</code> inspect supplied choices; null means no explicit choice.
        <code>resetEnabled()</code> chooses the built-in false default, while <code>useConfiguredEnabled()</code> restores inheritance.
        Replace the complete group with <code>withDebugConfig(...)</code>, edit existing choices through <code>getDebugConfig().toBuilder()</code>,
        or restore all inherited choices with <code>useConfiguredDebugConfig()</code>. Omitted fields inherit the factory snapshot.
        <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getDebugConfig()</code> exposes the resolved choices.</p>
      <pre><code class="small">// skip actually sending email, just log it
mailerBuilder.withTransportModeLoggingOnly(true);
</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="small">// custom SSL connection factory (note: breaks setups with authenticated proxy!)
mailerBuilder.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
    .withCustomSSLFactoryClass("org.mypackage.MySSLFactory")
    // or an already constructed factory; an instance takes precedence over the class
    .withCustomSSLFactoryInstance(mySSLFactoryInstance)
    .build());
</code></pre>
      <p>On <code>TlsConfig</code> and its builder, inspect the instance with <code>getCustomSSLFactoryInstance()</code>, or inspect the class name with
        <code>getCustomSSLFactoryClass()</code>. Use <code>clearCustomSSLFactoryInstance()</code> to return to the configured class, or
        <code>clearCustomSSLFactoryClass()</code> to remove that class choice. Clearing one leaves the other intact; configuration locks still apply.</p>
      <p>If your custom factory fails, the connection attempt fails too when Simple Java Mail creates the Angus Session. It does not quietly switch to
        the standard factory, which might not use your client certificate or custom certificate checks. To deliberately allow that fallback, use the
        existing advanced property:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback", "true");</code></pre>
      <p>The property-file and Spring key is <code>simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback</code>.
        For SMTPS, replace <code>mail.smtp</code> with <code>mail.smtps</code> in either form. Only enable this if the standard factory is acceptable.
        See <a href="security.html#section-custom-ssl-factory">custom SSL factories</a> for connection cleanup and module-access details.</p>
      <pre><code class="small">
// change email validation strategy
ValidationConfig.Builder validation = ValidationConfig.builder()
    .withEmailValidator(
        JMail.strictValidator()
            .requireOnlyTopLevelDomains(TopLevelDomain.DOT_COM)
            .withRule(email -> email.localPart().startsWith("allowed")));
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.build());

// restore the default strict JMail address validator:
validation.resetEmailValidator();
// clear the JMail address policy; keep completeness, encoded-word and CRLF checks:
validation.clearEmailValidator();
mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.build());
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">
// set custom properties
mailerBuilder.withProperties(new Properties());
mailerBuilder.withProperties(new HashMap());
mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.smtp.timeout", 10000);

// or directly modify the internal Session instance:
mailer.getSession().getProperties().setProperty("mail.smtp.timeout", "10000");
</code></pre>
      <p>The builder and <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig()</code> return separate property containers for inspection. Editing those returned
        containers does not reconfigure either object. Use builder methods before construction, or explicitly access the Session for an advanced
        runtime change. Object-valued provider settings, such as a socket factory, retain their supplied identity.</p>
      <p><code>mailerBuilder.withProperties(properties)</code> copies only entries stored directly in that Java <code>Properties</code> object,
        not values inherited from its defaults. Add any inherited value explicitly with <code>withProperty(...)</code> if the Session needs it.
        This differs from <code>ConfigLoader.builder().withProperties(...)</code>, which includes inherited string defaults when loading factory settings.</p>
      <p>For recipient-rejection handling, prefer the Email API over a raw provider property:
        <code>SmtpDeliveryOptions.Builder.withSendingToAcceptedRecipients(true)</code> continues for accepted recipients, and <code>false</code> withholds content after
        a known recipient rejection. Configure <code>simplejavamail.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients</code> for an ordinary Email default, or
        <code>simplejavamail.locked.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients</code> to keep the choice fixed within this factory. Ordinary defaults can be
        overridden or suppressed; a lock cannot. See <a href="analyzing-send-results.html#section-partial-send">both choices and their resulting receipts</a>,
        including why an advanced <code>sendpartial=true</code> property conflicts with an Email choosing false.</p>
<pre><code class="small">
/* Secure TLS defaults. These calls are optional. */
TlsConfig.Builder tls = TlsConfig.builder()
    .trustingAllHosts(false)
    .verifyingServerIdentity(true);
mailerBuilder.withTlsConfig(tls.build());

// preferred for private PKI: add the issuing CA to the JVM trust store
// narrow compatibility exception if that is not possible:
tls.trustingSSLHosts("smtp.internal.example");
// broad compatibility escape hatch; avoid in production:
tls.trustingAllHosts(true);

// clear or reset trust exceptions
tls.clearTrustedSSLHosts().resetTrustingAllHosts();
mailerBuilder.withTlsConfig(tls.build());
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">
// compatibility escape hatch; disables hostname verification
TlsConfig.Builder tls = mailerBuilder.getTlsConfig().toBuilder()
    .verifyingServerIdentity(false);
mailerBuilder.withTlsConfig(tls.build());
// restore the secure default
mailerBuilder.withTlsConfig(tls.resetVerifyingServerIdentity().build());
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="small">// explicit worker settings apply with or without the batch module
mailerBuilder.withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
    .withThreadPoolSize(3)
    // Duration.ZERO: core threads stay alive; positive values retire idle workers
    .withKeepAliveTime(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
    // optional bound on waiting async tasks, separate from worker count
    .withQueueCapacity(100)
    .rejectWhenFull()
    .build());

// completely replace the thread pool executor with your own
// replacing the group discards the earlier explicit built-in settings
// configure your executor's queue yourself
mailerBuilder.withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig.builder()
    .withExecutorService(new MyAwesomeCustomThreadPoolExecutor())
    .build());
</code></pre>
      <p>Unspecified worker settings retain the one-worker default without batch and ten-worker default with batch.
        Java session, executor and pool time settings use <code>Duration</code>; the corresponding properties and CLI arguments still use
        numeric milliseconds. These migrated settings reject fractional milliseconds and overflow. Application-owned executors keep their own tuning.</p>
      <h4 id="socket-timeouts">Socket timeouts</h4>
      <pre><code class="small">ConnectionConfig.Builder connection = ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withSessionTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .withConnectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
    .withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(45));
mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(connection.build());
// connect: 10 seconds, read: 45 seconds, write: 30 seconds</code></pre>
      <p>The common setter writes the same three choices as the individual setters. Calling
        <code>connection.withSessionTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(20))</code> afterward selects 20 seconds for all three.
        <code>resetConnectTimeout()</code>, <code>resetReadTimeout()</code> and <code>resetWriteTimeout()</code> select the built-in
        60-second value for just that operation. Their <code>useConfigured...</code> companions restore captured inheritance.
        <code>resetSessionTimeout()</code> and <code>useConfiguredSessionTimeout()</code> apply those operations to all three.</p>
      <p>The CLI uses numeric milliseconds and applies the same setter order:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-text small">--mailer:withSessionTimeout 30000 --mailer:withConnectTimeout 10000 --mailer:withReadTimeout 45000</code></pre>
      <p><code>--mailer:withWriteTimeout</code> adjusts writes independently. The corresponding CLI reset options are
        <code>--mailer:resetConnectTimeout</code>, <code>--mailer:resetReadTimeout</code> and <code>--mailer:resetWriteTimeout</code>.</p>
      <p>For a Session created by Simple Java Mail, each timeout uses this order: an explicit typed choice in <code>ConnectionConfig</code>,
        an additional Jakarta Mail timeout property, an operation-specific SJM property, the shared session-timeout property, then the
        built-in 60-second fallback. For example, raw <code>mail.smtp.connectiontimeout=10000</code> and <code>mail.smtp.timeout=30000</code>
        give connect/read/write values of 10/30/60 seconds with otherwise default configuration. An explicit typed read timeout of 45 seconds
        changes only the read value.</p>
      <p>A Session you supply keeps each original value until you select that operation through a typed choice, raw overlay or applicable lock.
        Ordinary SJM timeout defaults do not replace its settings. Mandatory locks apply above ordinary precedence: an individual lock
        constrains its operation, while a shared session-timeout lock constrains all three. Conflicting choices or lock requirements are rejected.</p>
      <p>When write timeouts are enabled, Simple Java Mail shares one background timeout-monitoring thread across the connections from each Angus Session
        it creates, rather than using a thread for every connection. This thread watches for writes that take too long; it does not send emails.
        It stops when the last connection is closed. Returning a connection to the pool keeps it open, so the monitoring thread stays available too.
        A later connection starts the thread again when needed.</p>
      <p>If you supply your own scheduler through <code>mail.smtp.executor.writetimeout</code> or <code>mail.smtps.executor.writetimeout</code>,
        Simple Java Mail does not shut it down; close it when your application is finished with it.
        Sessions you supply yourself and other mail providers keep their own timeout handling. These timeouts cover individual socket operations,
        not the total time taken to send an email.</p>
		  <pre><code class="small">// bind the outgoing SMTP socket to a local/source address
// the local port is advanced and usually should be omitted
mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withLocalBindAddress("203.0.113.10")
    .build());
mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withLocalBindAddress("203.0.113.10", 25252)
    .build());
</code></pre>
		  <pre><code class="small">// identify the SMTP client hostname sent in EHLO/HELO
// useful for corporate relay policy, logging, tracing and diagnostics
mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withSmtpClientHostname("orders-service.prod.example.com")
    .build());
mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .clearSmtpClientHostname()
    .build());
</code></pre>
		  <pre><code class="small">// message policy uses Email templates, including signing
Email signingDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .signWithDomainKey(defaultDkimConfig)
    .buildEmail();
mailerBuilder.withEmailDefaults(signingDefaults);
// individual emails can still provide their own DKIM config
</code></pre>
		  <pre><code class="small">// change the default sending logic to your own approach
mailerBuilder.withCustomMailer(yourOwnCustomMailerImpl); // send emails, test connections
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-legacy-smtp-content" id="section-legacy-smtp-content" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Known tolerant legacy SMTP servers</h3>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>Reuse one Mailer for ASCII and international mail; the bundled managed Angus transport negotiates each message separately.
        Only enable this compatibility option after verifying that the server and onward delivery route preserve internationalized addresses,
        raw UTF-8 headers and raw 8-bit bodies without advertising the corresponding extensions. It defaults to false:</p>
<pre><code class="small">mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withLegacySmtpContentSupport(true)
    .build());
boolean enabled = mailer.getOperationalConfig().isLegacySmtpContentSupportEnabled();</code></pre>
      <p>This does not relax TLS, REQUIRETLS, explicit DSN or content-safety checks. An accepted submission does not prove downstream delivery.
        In a shared cluster, the selected Session's configuration supplies this permission, not the Mailer you happened to call.
        Read the <a href="features.html#section-international-mail">international-mail workflow and provider boundaries</a> before enabling it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-proxy-bridge-ports" id="section-proxy-bridge-ports" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 socks=true}}Authenticated proxy bridge ports{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Jakarta Mail cannot authenticate to a SOCKS proxy by itself. For authenticated proxies, Simple Java Mail starts a small local bridge that
        handles the proxy login and relays the connection. The bridge listens only on the JVM's loopback address and stops when its Mailer has no
        active proxy operations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Concurrent work through one Mailer shares that Mailer's bridge. By default the bridge asks the operating system for an available loopback port
        every time it starts. Separate authenticated-proxy Mailers can therefore run at the same time without coordinating ports. Anonymous SOCKS
        proxies do not use a bridge.
      </p>
      <p>
        Simple Java Mail writes the selected port to the effective Session before opening the SMTP connection. The value is available as
        <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.socks.port</code> or <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtps.socks.port</code>
        and can change when a stopped bridge starts again. Use <code class="inline language-java">SocksProxyConfig.Builder.withBridgePort(...)</code>, or the
        <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.proxy.socks5bridge.port</code> property, only when you need a fixed positive port.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "smtp-user", "smtp-password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
        .withHost("proxy.example.com")
        .withPort(1080)
        .withUsername("proxy-user")
        .withPassword("proxy-password")
        .build())
    .buildMailer();

// Only if the local bridge must use a fixed port:
Mailer fixedPortMailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "smtp-user", "smtp-password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
        .withHost("proxy.example.com")
        .withPort(1080)
        .withUsername("proxy-user")
        .withPassword("proxy-password")
        .withBridgePort(1081)
        .build())
    .buildMailer();
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-config-properties" id="section-config-properties" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Properties files</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Use <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()</code> when you want the conventional setup. On its first call, it reads an
        optional <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.properties</code> resource from the classpath, then environment variables, then
        JVM system properties. It keeps that immutable snapshot for later calls and never consults Spring.
      </p>
      <p>
        For explicit application-owned configuration, add sources to a new <code class="inline language-java">ConfigLoader</code> from lowest to highest
        priority and call <code class="inline language-java">load()</code>. A later non-blank value wins. A blank value is treated as absent, so it does
        not hide a useful value from an earlier source.
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-properties small"># Earlier source
simplejavamail.smtp.username=relay-user

# Later source: does not clear relay-user
simplejavamail.smtp.username=</code></pre>
      <p>Null map values and whitespace-only strings are absent too. To deliberately remove a captured SMTP username in Java, use
        <code>clearSMTPServerUsername()</code>; the password remains a separate choice. Clearing both stored credentials requires
        <code>clearSMTPServerPassword()</code> as well, and configuration locks still apply. See the
        <a href="#configuration-operations">operation table</a> for the distinction between clearing and restoring configuration.</p>
      <p>
        Only the winning value is parsed. A malformed lower-priority value therefore causes no error when a valid higher-priority source replaces it:
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Lower priority: simplejavamail.properties
simplejavamail.smtp.port=not-a-number
                    ↓ overridden before parsing
Higher priority: environment variable
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
                    ↓ parsed
Loaded SMTP port: 587</code></pre>
      <p>
        The conventional short form is equivalent to:
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withClasspathResource("simplejavamail.properties")
    .withEnvironmentVariables()
    .withSystemProperties()
    .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);</code></pre>
      <p>
        Only add environment and system-property sources when you want them. Explicit loaders do not silently read either one. Calls made directly on a
        Mailer or email builder override the snapshot values copied into that builder.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
      Compose the sources your application owns:
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withClasspathResource("mail-base.properties")
    .withPropertiesFile(
        "deployment file",
        Path.of("config", "simplejavamail.properties"))
    .withProperties("runtime overrides", runtimeOverrides)
    .withMap("local overrides", localOverrides)
    .withSource(secretManagerSource)
    .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);</code></pre>
      <p>
        A <code class="inline language-java">withPropertiesFile(Path)</code> source is sampled whenever <code class="inline language-java">load()</code>
        runs, and Simple Java Mail closes the stream it opens. <code class="inline language-java">withInputStream(...)</code> still consumes and closes the
        supplied stream immediately. Maps, <code class="inline language-java">Properties</code>, and custom sources are sampled at load time. The snapshot
        detaches their values before returning.
      </p>
      <p>A Java <code>Properties</code> input includes its inherited string defaults, including locked settings. Direct entries take precedence
        over those defaults, and the combined input is sampled again on each <code>load()</code>. The usual source ordering and validation still apply.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        A custom <code class="inline language-java">ConfigSource</code> returns a stable diagnostic name and a map keyed by the canonical
        <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.*</code> names. Strict sources reject unknown keys. Process environment and system
        sources are deliberately non-strict because most of their entries belong to other software.
      </p>
      <p>
        Secret lookup, decryption, and refresh policy belong in your application or configuration framework. Return the already decrypted values from
        the source. Simple Java Mail validates and copies them, but does not add a secret-encryption format of its own.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">ConfigSource secretManagerSource = new ConfigSource() {
    public String getName() {
        return "application secret manager";
    }

    public Map&lt;String, ?&gt; getProperties() {
        return secretManager.readMailSettings();
    }
};</code></pre>
      <p>
        Validation errors name the source and property key without printing the configured value. A snapshot's
        <code class="inline language-java">toString()</code> redacts passwords, key material, and arbitrary Session properties.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-config-snapshot" id="section-config-snapshot" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>When changes take effect</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        A <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code> is a detached, thread-safe snapshot. Changing a source after
        <code class="inline language-java">load()</code>, or loading another snapshot, does not reconfigure an existing factory, builder,
        <code class="inline language-java">Email</code>, <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code>, or mail session.
      </p>
      <p>
        To change configuration, build a new snapshot and factory, then request fresh builders from it. You can use a prior snapshot as the first source
        with <code class="inline language-java">withConfig(previous)</code> when the replacement should override only part of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Inspect captured values with <code class="inline language-java">get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT)</code>, <code>hasProperty(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT)</code>, and
        <code class="inline language-java">getPropertySource(...)</code>. <code class="inline language-java">asMap()</code> and the wildcard maps it
        contains are unmodifiable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Static inbound methods on <code class="inline language-java">EmailConverter</code> use the conventional default snapshot. Use
        <code class="inline language-java">mail.converter()</code> when parsing should produce builders backed by an explicit factory snapshot.
        For example, <code>mail.converter().fromEml(emlPath).toEmailBuilder()</code> keeps that snapshot without restricting the supported conversion routes.
        See <a href="/features.html#section-converting">source-first conversion</a> for the complete shape and receive-side options.
      </p>
      <p>
        When replacing a <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code>, let any work already using the old instance finish and then
        <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-mailer-lifecycle">close it</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig productionConfig = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withProperties("production", productionProperties)
    .load();
SimpleJavaMail productionMail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(productionConfig);
Mailer productionMailer = productionMail.mailerBuilder().buildMailer();

SimpleJavaMailConfig failoverConfig = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withProperties("failover", failoverProperties)
    .load();
SimpleJavaMail failoverMail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(failoverConfig);
Mailer failoverMailer = failoverMail.mailerBuilder().buildMailer();

// Existing objects still use productionConfig.
// Send new work through failoverMailer, let old work finish, then:
productionMailer.close();
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-typed-config-keys" id="section-typed-config-keys" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Read configuration values in Java</h3>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Each <code>ConfigKeys</code> key carries its Java value type. Use the same key for reading, presence/source inspection
      and explicit/configured/fallback selection. The compiler rejects a port assigned to String or a String fallback for an Integer key.
      These are captured input values; built-in and derived Mailer defaults are resolved by the builders.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withMap("deployment", Map.of(
        "simplejavamail.smtp.port", "587",
        "simplejavamail.defaults.sessiontimeoutmillis", "1500"))
    .load();

Integer port = config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT);
Duration timeout = config.get(ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT);
Integer selectedPort = config.valueOrProperty(null, ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT, 25);
String source = config.getPropertySource(ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT);
boolean configured = config.hasProperty(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT);
// String wrong = config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT); // does not compile</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      All time-valued key reads return <code>Duration</code>. Existing numeric-millisecond input names still accept their
      integer representation and retain signed Integer bounds and whole-millisecond precision; ISO-duration inputs keep
      their existing precision and validation. A Java read of 1500 above returns <code>Duration.ofMillis(1500)</code>.
      Dynamic map inputs do not change units or gain a different parser because the key's Java view is Duration.
      Socket timeouts expose <code>ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT</code>, <code>CONNECT_TIMEOUT</code>, <code>READ_TIMEOUT</code> and
      <code>WRITE_TIMEOUT</code>. Reading a missing per-operation key returns null even when the shared key is present;
      the owning Mailer resolves that fallback when it creates a Session.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Missing scalars and aggregate configuration maps return null: an absent key does not fabricate an empty configured
      map or a runtime default. Present maps retain complete types—<code>Map&lt;String, String&gt;</code> for extra properties,
      <code>Map&lt;UUID, ConnectionPoolClusterConfig&gt;</code> for clusters, and <code>Map&lt;String, SimpleJavaMailConfig&gt;</code>
      for named Mailers—and are detached and unmodifiable. This configuration convention is separate from operation-result collections.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Map&lt;String, String&gt; extras = config.valueOrProperty(
    null, ConfigKeys.EXTRA_PROPERTIES, Map.of()); // application fallback; the absent key stays absent

SimpleJavaMailConfig copied = ConfigLoader.builder().withConfig(config).load();
Duration sameTimeout = copied.get(ConfigKeys.SESSION_TIMEOUT);</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      <code>valueOrProperty</code> keeps a nonempty explicit value, otherwise the configured value, otherwise its supplied
      fallback. An empty String is absent; whitespace Strings, zero and false retain their usual meaning. Explicit and
      fallback objects remain application-owned. Neither a read nor this helper changes sources or bypasses locks.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code>ConfigLoader.Property</code> and <code>config.asMap()</code> remain available for dynamic integration. That
      heterogeneous map retains parser values, including Integer milliseconds for the numeric timeout keys; ordinary Java
      reads use <code>get(ConfigKey&lt;T&gt;)</code>. Typed reads and raw maps can expose credentials: use
      <code>getDiagnostics()</code> for safe logging and source provenance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-environment-variables" id="section-environment-variables" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Environment variable names</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        For the fixed properties listed below, uppercase the property name and replace each dot with an underscore. There is no additional relaxed
        binding: the name comes directly from the documented property key.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two wildcard namespaces are different: <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.*</code> and
        <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.*</code> are scanned as literal names. An environment
        variable such as <code class="inline language-text">SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_EXTRAPROPERTIES_MAIL_SMTP_TIMEOUT</code> is therefore not recognized.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some process launchers can supply literal lowercase names containing dots, but support varies between shells, containers, and operating systems.
        For wildcard settings, prefer a property file, an exact dotted JVM <code class="inline language-text">-D</code> system property, or the Java API.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties">SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_TRANSPORTSTRATEGY=SMTP_TLS
SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_DEFAULTS_FROM_ADDRESS=sender@example.com
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-available-properties" id="section-available-properties" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Available properties</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Most mailer settings have a property equivalent, so each environment can use different values without changing application code.
      </p>
	  <p>
		<code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls</code> only affects the optional STARTTLS upgrade used by plain
		<code class="inline language-text">SMTP</code>. It does not weaken <code class="inline language-text">SMTP_TLS</code>, OAuth2 transport, or
		<code class="inline language-text">SMTPS</code>. An explicit <code class="inline language-java">TlsConfig.Builder.withOpportunisticTLS(...)</code> call wins over the property.
	  </p>
	  <p>
		For <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private_key_file_or_data</code>, prefix a path with
		<code class="inline language-properties">file:</code> or inline Base64-encoded key bytes with
		<code class="inline language-properties">base64:</code>. Unprefixed values retain their legacy path-or-UTF-8-data behavior.
	  </p>
	  <p>
		The embedded-image flags describe their exact route: <code class="inline language-properties">outside.base.url</code> controls URL resolution and
		<code class="inline language-properties">outside.base.classpath</code> controls classpath resolution.
	  </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties"># Debugging and transport
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug=true
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug.out=SLF4J
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS
simplejavamail.transport.mode.logging.only=false
simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls=false

# Optional OpenTelemetry; the application supplies its global SDK or an explicit Java integration
simplejavamail.telemetry.enabled=false
simplejavamail.telemetry.smtp.endpointattributes=false

# SMTP destination, protocol identity and local/source binding
simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.default.com
simplejavamail.smtp.port=587
simplejavamail.smtp.username=username
simplejavamail.smtp.password=password
simplejavamail.smtp.clienthostname=orders-service.prod.example.com
# Opt in only for a verified legacy server and onward delivery route
simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport=false
simplejavamail.smtp.localaddress=203.0.113.10
simplejavamail.smtp.localport=25252

# Optional sending limits; these example rates are not defaults. Omit a rule's count and period to disable it.
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.group=company-account
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.messages.limit=30
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.messages.period=PT1M
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.recipients.limit=100
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.recipients.period=PT1M
simplejavamail.smtp.ratelimit.allowbursts=false

# Optional safely refused mail-send retries. Both attempts and delay are required; omitted means disabled.
# simplejavamail.smtp.retry.attempts=3
# simplejavamail.smtp.retry.delay=PT30S
# simplejavamail.smtp.retry.backoffmultiplier=2
# simplejavamail.smtp.retry.maximumdelay=PT5M
# simplejavamail.smtp.retry.jitter=0.2
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.waitingcapacity=100
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.maximummessagesize=33554432
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.retry.memorylimit=134217728

# Validation, SSL and proxying
simplejavamail.disable.all.clientvalidation=false
simplejavamail.custom.sslfactory.class=org.mypackage.ssl.MySSLSocketFactoryClass
simplejavamail.proxy.host=proxy.default.com
simplejavamail.proxy.port=1080
simplejavamail.proxy.username=username proxy
simplejavamail.proxy.password=password proxy
# 0 lets the operating system select an available loopback bridge port
simplejavamail.proxy.socks5bridge.port=0
# Shared socket timeout fallback, in milliseconds
simplejavamail.defaults.sessiontimeoutmillis=60000
# Optional per-operation overrides, also in milliseconds
simplejavamail.defaults.connecttimeoutmillis=10000
simplejavamail.defaults.readtimeoutmillis=30000
simplejavamail.defaults.writetimeoutmillis=45000
# Optional total send budget (ISO-8601); omitted means disabled
simplejavamail.defaults.mailsend.timeout=PT30S
simplejavamail.defaults.trustallhosts=false
simplejavamail.defaults.trustedhosts=192.168.1.122;mymailserver.com;ix55432y
simplejavamail.defaults.verifyserveridentity=true

# Email defaults
simplejavamail.defaults.subject=Sweet News
simplejavamail.defaults.from.name=From Default
simplejavamail.defaults.from.address=from@default.com
simplejavamail.defaults.replyto.name=Reply-To Default
simplejavamail.defaults.replyto.address=reply-to@default.com
simplejavamail.defaults.bounceto.name=Bounce-To Default
simplejavamail.defaults.bounceto.address=bounce-to@default.com
simplejavamail.defaults.to.name=To Default
simplejavamail.defaults.to.address=to@default.com
simplejavamail.defaults.cc.name=CC Default
simplejavamail.defaults.cc.address=cc@default.com
simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.name=
simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.address=bcc1@default.com;bcc2@default.com

# Delivery Status Notification defaults
# Shared Email fallback; explicit recipient/group NOTIFY preferences take precedence.
# ENVID is generated per send when DSN is supported; fixingEnvelopeId(...) supplies an optional fixed value on the Email.
simplejavamail.defaults.delivery.status.notification.notify=FAILURE,DELAY
simplejavamail.defaults.delivery.status.notification.return.option=HEADERS_ONLY
simplejavamail.defaults.requiretls=true
# Optional recipient-rejection choice; omitted leaves existing provider behavior in control:
simplejavamail.defaults.sendtoacceptedrecipients=true

# Content-Transfer-Encoding defaults
simplejavamail.defaults.content.transfer.encoding=QUOTED_PRINTABLE
simplejavamail.defaults.body.text.content.transfer.encoding=BIT7
simplejavamail.defaults.body.html.content.transfer.encoding=BASE_64
simplejavamail.defaults.body.calendar.content.transfer.encoding=QUOTED_PRINTABLE

# Pooled-delivery executor defaults
simplejavamail.defaults.poolsize=10
simplejavamail.defaults.poolsize.keepalivetime=2000

# Execution mode is chosen in Java: mailer.sync() or mailer.async(); there is no mode property.
# These settings configure the async executor's queue, not a Mailer-wide async default.
# Optional async queue controls (default capacity is -1, meaning unbounded)
simplejavamail.defaults.async.queue.capacity=100
simplejavamail.defaults.async.queue.overflowpolicy=REJECT
# Positive caller-side admission wait; used only by WAIT_FOR_CAPACITY
simplejavamail.defaults.async.queue.waittimeoutmillis=1000

# Connection-pool defaults
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusterkey.uuid=38400000-8cf0-11bd-b23e-10b96e4ef00d
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.coresize=0
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.maxsize=4
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.claimtimeout.millis=10000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.expireafter.millis=5000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.loadbalancing.strategy=ROUND_ROBIN

# Per-cluster defaults
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.clusterkey.uuid=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000301
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.coresize=0
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.maxsize=3
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.claimtimeout.millis=30000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.expireafter.millis=600000
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.orders.loadbalancing.strategy=RANDOM_ACCESS
simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.clusters.00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000302.maxsize=8

# S/MIME defaults
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore=my_keystore.pkcs12
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore_password=keystore_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key_alias=key_alias
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key_password=key_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.algorithm=SHA3-256withECDSA
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.certificate=x509inStandardPEM.crt
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.key_encapsulation_algorithm=RSA_OAEP_SHA384
# AES is recommended; DES_EDE3_CBC remains available for legacy recipient compatibility.
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.cipher=AES256_CBC

# DKIM defaults
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private_key_file_or_data=file:my_dkim_key.der
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.selector=dkim1
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.signing_domain=your-domain.com
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.use_length_param=false
# Omit header exclusions to keep the default signing list, including From.
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.header_canonicalization=RELAXED
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.body_canonicalization=RELAXED
simplejavamail.dkim.signing.algorithm=SHA256_WITH_RSA

# Embedded image dynamic resolution
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.enable.dir=true
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.enable.url=false
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.enable.classpath=true
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.base.dir=/var/opt/static
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.base.url=
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.base.classpath=/static
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.outside.base.dir=true
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.outside.base.classpath=false
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.outside.base.url=false
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.mustbesuccesful=true
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

	<section>
		<div class="wide">
			<p>
				Spring applications use these same property names. The <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-profiles">Spring integration guide</a> explains
				profile handling, dynamic keys, and the configuration metadata available to compatible IDEs.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="view">
			<p>
				Then there are extra properties which will directly go on the internal Session object when
				building a Mailer instance.
			</p>
			<p>
				They use the same precedence as the other settings: system properties win over environment variables, and environment variables win over
				property-file values.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.my.extra.property=value
simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.ssl.socketFactory.class=org.mypackage.MySSLSocketFactory
simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.timeout=30000
</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>


	<a href="#section-config-mailer" id="section-config-mailer" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	<h2>Mailer level email defaults and overrides</h2>

	<section>
		<p class="wide">
			Property files, environment variables, and system properties supply defaults captured in your factory's configuration snapshot.
			Use Email templates when different Mailers need different message settings, such as a sender or signing configuration.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			To/Cc/Bcc address defaults use the same complete-list interpretation as Java composition: quoted names, comma/semicolon separators
			and RFC group members are supported in source order. A configured name applies to every parsed mailbox;
			without it, embedded names are retained. From and bounce defaults remain single-mailbox choices. Parsing keeps malformed nonempty text
			for the configured send-time validator; it does not check delivery or query DNS. See the
			<a href="features.html#section-recipient-builders">recipient name and list examples</a>.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			A supplied defaults template replaces the property-derived message defaults entirely; missing fields are not filled from properties.
			To retain those values, first use <code>buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides()</code> to materialize them, then edit a copy.
			Ordinary <code>buildEmail()</code> leaves defaults unapplied. Templates can be incomplete, including a DKIM signing template without a sender;
			the Mailer validates the prepared message before sending.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			Sending, rehearsing or exporting the original <a href="features.html#section-exact-eml">exact Email</a> bypasses ordinary message templates. Its supplied bytes are already final,
			so Mailer defaults, overrides, configured content validation, and security transformations cannot change them. Put any required visible headers in the
			EML before building the exact Email, and configure only its SMTP envelope on the exact builder.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			Explicitly calling <code>mailer.getEmailGovernance().produceEmailApplyingDefaultsAndOverrides(exactEmail)</code> applies ordinary defaults and
			overrides to the parsed view and returns a new composed Email. The supplied exact Email remains unchanged. The composed result does not guarantee
			the original EML bytes or the validity of signatures already on the source; reuse the original exact Email when those bytes must be preserved.
		</p>
		<div class="wide">
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Email yourServerLevelDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
	/* set your defaults here */
	.buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides(); // complete it from this factory's snapshot

Mailer adminServer = mail.mailerBuilder()
	// add SMTP settings
	.withEmailDefaults(yourServerLevelDefaults)
	.withEmailOverrides(yourServerLevelOverrides)
	.buildMailer();
</code></pre>
		</div>

		<p class="wide">
			One use case for this is when you have multiple mailer instances, each for a separate SMTP server clustered together (see
			<a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-clustering">clustering</a>), you may want to define defaults or overrides for a specific server. You can do that by defining
			a reference Email instance and set it as <em>defaults</em> or <em>overrides</em> parameter.
		</p>
		<div class="wide">
			<pre><code class="language-java">// for example: force FROM to be always the same for the
// specific SMTP adminServer from the previous example:
Email yourServerLevelOverrides = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("Admin", "admin@yourcompany.com")
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wide">
			<h4 id="configuration-precedence">Which scope supplies the value?</h4>
			<table><thead><tr><th>Scope or value</th><th>Resolution</th></tr></thead><tbody>
				<tr><td>Property sources</td><td>Later non-blank values win. Blank or null entries do not erase an earlier value.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Single values and complete policy values</td><td>Applicable override, then submitted Email, then applicable default. Compound crypto configurations are selected as a whole.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Recipients, Reply-To, delivery-recipient overrides, attachments and embedded images</td><td>Add missing override entries, retain every submitted entry, then add missing default entries. Existing duplicates and their order remain intact.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Headers</td><td>Combine names case-insensitively. For one name, an override replaces its entire value list; otherwise submitted values replace the default list.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Recipient certificates and NOTIFY preferences</td><td>Group policy constructs the recipient metadata. The resulting explicit recipient value wins over the governance-resolved Email fallback, including an Email override.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Configuration locks</td><td>Separate mandatory restrictions remain binding through ordinary clears, template replacement and suppression.</td></tr>
			</tbody></table>
			<p>The per-email exclusions below determine which ordinary template contributions apply. Sending, rehearsing and exporting the original exact Email
				preserve its final bytes without ordinary templates or security transformations. Compatible envelope locks still apply;
				locks that would require changing the preserved content are rejected.
				See the <a href="security.html#section-sending-smime">recipient certificate rules</a> for the separate group-policy choices.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="wide">
			<h4 id="repeated-email-completion">Completing an Email more than once</h4>
			<p>A completed Email can be completed or sent again, including through another Mailer. Each pass can fill missing scalar values and apply
				the selected Mailer's overrides. Completion keeps your suppression choices and always enforces applicable locks. It does not freeze the message.</p>
			<p>Completion carries outgoing content and settings, including S/MIME, OpenPGP and DKIM configuration; protection is applied later when MIME
				is prepared. Historical cryptographic results and original protected bytes, <a href="delivery-reports.html#trust-and-preservation">received delivery reports</a>
				and their nested facts, decrypted-attachment inspection lists and retained S/MIME
				signed-content views are not copied into the completed Email. Inspect the original converted Email for those results. Ordinary copying and
				supported Java serialization preserve input history. Content already present in ordinary bodies, headers and resources remains outgoing content.</p>
			<p>Additive templates contribute only values not already present. For example, supplied recipients <code>[A, A, B]</code>, overrides
				<code>[A, C, C]</code> and defaults <code>[B, D, D]</code> produce <code>[C, A, A, B, D]</code>. Reapplying those templates keeps that
				order. Recipient matching includes the name, address, To/Cc/Bcc role, certificate and notification preferences, accounting for missing
				names supplied by mandatory locks. Explicit conflicting names still fail validation.</p>
			<p>Attachments and images match only when their metadata and recognized source agree. Reusing the same resource or DataSource is recognized;
				library-owned paths and byte arrays can also be compared without opening a stream. Different filenames, descriptions, Content-IDs or
				encoding choices remain distinct. Duplicate detection does not read files or remote content. Different opaque sources remain separate,
				even if their names and MIME types match; serialization or reconstruction can lose recognizable source identity.</p>
			<p>For ordinary sends, use <code>buildEmail()</code> and let the selected destination apply its settings. If you send a completed result,
				its materialized values are now supplied values: another Mailer's default sender cannot replace an existing sender, but an override can.
				Headers and complete policies retain the rules above. The original exact Email and frozen retry content keep their separate preservation contracts.</p>
		</div>
		<p class="wide">
			A template can also supply the message being forwarded. A default fills a missing forwarded message; an override replaces the one supplied
			on the Email. Use <code>EmailProperty.MAIL_TO_FORWARD</code> with the per-field opt-outs below to skip that template contribution.
			The subject still follows its own precedence. The selected <code>MimeMessage</code> remains borrowed, so keep it available and unchanged
			while the Email is being used; applying templates does not read or copy its body. Sending, rehearsing and exporting an exact Email still bypass ordinary templates.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			Repeated <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code> calls replace the template. <code>useConfiguredEmailDefaults()</code> restores the snapshot-derived
			defaults. <code>withoutEmailDefaults()</code> omits ordinary message defaults while retaining configuration locks; per-email opt-outs remain available below.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			To remove only default DKIM signing while preserving other property defaults, materialize the defaults, copy that Email, call
			<code>clearDkim()</code>, and pass the result to <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code>. Materialization still reads any configured signing key.
			The <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#dkim-defaults">migration example</a> shows both replacing and removing the default signature.
		</p>
		<div class="wide">
			<p>Clearing a submitted Email's local DKIM choice has a different scope: ordinary templates can supply signing again.
				Exclude those template contributions separately when that is intended:</p>
			<pre><code class="language-java">EmailPopulatingBuilder draft = mail.emailBuilder()
    .copying(original)
    .clearDkim();
// The local choice is gone; ordinary signing defaults or overrides can still apply.

draft.withTemplateOptions(draft.getTemplateOptions().toBuilder()
    .dontApplyDefaultValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
    .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
    .build());</code></pre>
			<p>Editing the existing options retains unrelated exclusions. This suppresses ordinary DKIM template contributions for that Email;
				it does not remove mandatory configuration locks or change the original Email.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="view">
			<p>
				To keep defaults or overrides away from one email, configure that after whichever builder starter you used.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions.builder()
        .ignoringDefaults()
        .ignoringOverrides()
        .build())
    // add the email content
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="view">
			<p>
				You can even exclude specific fields from the defaults or overrides if you want to make very specific exceptions.
			</p>
			<p>Use <code>EmailProperty.ALL_RECIPIENTS</code> to exclude ordinary To, Cc and Bcc additions together, or choose one recipient type
				to exclude only that type. Your Email's own recipients remain. Delivery-recipient overrides use the separate
				<code>OVERRIDE_RECEIVERS</code> selector, and none of these exclusions removes recipients required by locked properties.</p>
			<p>On <code>EmailTemplateOptions.Builder</code>, repeated suppression calls accumulate exclusions; an empty call changes nothing. Use <code>allowDefaultValueFor(...)</code>
				or <code>allowOverrideValueFor(...)</code> to remove selected exclusions, and <code>clearDefaultValueSuppressions()</code>
				or <code>clearOverrideValueSuppressions()</code> to remove all exclusions in that family. These edits leave global
				<code>ignoringDefaults()</code>/<code>ignoringOverrides()</code> choices and configuration locks unchanged.</p>
			<p>A calendar's method and text belong together. Excluding either <code>CALENDAR_METHOD</code> or <code>CALENDAR_TEXT</code>
				skips that source's whole calendar. An override exclusion lets the Email's own calendar, or otherwise its default calendar, apply;
				a default exclusion leaves overrides eligible. Your Email's own calendar remains. If you excluded both selectors, allowing only one
				leaves the other exclusion active. Calendar transfer encoding is a separate choice.</p>
		</div>

		<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">emailBuilder
	.withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions.builder()
	    .dontApplyDefaultValueFor(EmailProperty.FROM_RECIPIENT, EmailProperty.REPLYTO_RECIPIENT)
	    .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
	    .build())
</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>


  <a href="#section-combine-config" id="section-combine-config" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Combining everything for multiple environments</h2>

  <section>
    <div>
      <p>
        Let's set up configuration for a <strong>test</strong>, <strong>acceptance</strong> and <strong>production</strong> environment.
      </p>
      <h3>Properties for the environments</h3>
    </div>
    <div>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">#global default properties (simplejavamail.properties on classpath)

    # anonoymous SMTP inside 'safe' DMZ
    simplejavamail.smtp.host=dmz.smtp.candyshop.com
    simplejavamail.smtp.port=25

    # default sender and reply-to address
    simplejavamail.defaults.from.name=The Candy App
    simplejavamail.defaults.from.address=candyapp@candystore.com
    simplejavamail.defaults.replyto.name=Candystore Helpdesk
    simplejavamail.defaults.replyto.address=helpdesk@candystore.com
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">#overrides from TEST and UAT .../config/candystore/simplejavamail.properties

    # always send a copy to test archive
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.name=Archive TST UAT
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.address=test-archive@candyshop.com
</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties small">#overrides from PRODUCTION .../config/candystore/simplejavamail.properties

    # always send a copy to production archive
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.name=Archive PRODUCTION
    simplejavamail.defaults.bcc.address=prod-archive@candyshop.com

    # smtp server in production is protected
    simplejavamail.smtp.username=creamcake
    simplejavamail.smtp.password=crusty_l0llyp0p

    # sending mails in production must go through proxy
    simplejavamail.proxy.host=proxy.candyshop.com
    simplejavamail.proxy.port=1080
    simplejavamail.proxy.username=candyman
    simplejavamail.proxy.password=I has the sugarcanes!!1!
</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Now for the programmatic part</h3>

    <div>
      <p>
        Build the complete source list for the selected environment, then load one snapshot and create the factory that owns it. The order below goes
        from broad defaults to the most deployment-specific overrides.
      </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ConfigLoader loader = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withClasspathResource("simplejavamail.properties")
    .withPropertiesFile(
        "selected environment",
        Path.of("config", "candystore", "simplejavamail.properties"));

if (someSpecialCondition) {
    loader.withClasspathResource("special-override.properties");
}

Properties runtimeOverrides = new Properties();
runtimeOverrides.setProperty("simplejavamail.smtp.port", "2525");
SimpleJavaMailConfig config = loader
    .withProperties("runtime overrides", runtimeOverrides)
    .withEnvironmentVariables()
    .withSystemProperties()
    .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    // Direct builder values win over the snapshot.
    .withSMTPServerPort(587)
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .buildMailer();
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>


