  <a href="#section-locked-configuration" id="section-locked-configuration" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Keep centrally supplied settings fixed</h2>
  {{> components/pattern-examples topic="locked-configuration"}}
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>Suppose your application should always use the company relay and include an archive mailbox, while individual jobs choose their own
        subjects and recipients. Put the fixed values under <code>simplejavamail.locked.</code>, followed by the existing property's name.
        The value is the actual setting, not a true/false marker saying whether it is locked.</p>
      <p>Every Mailer created from the same factory inherits these restrictions. An ordinary property cannot replace its locked counterpart,
        even when the ordinary property comes from a higher-priority source. Competing locked entries still follow normal source precedence;
        a blank entry does not clear an earlier value. Supplying the same parsed value in Java is allowed; a conflicting customization fails.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Still customizable by application code:
simplejavamail.smtp.port=587

# Fixed for this factory:
simplejavamail.locked.smtp.host=relay.company.com
simplejavamail.locked.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS
simplejavamail.locked.defaults.bcc.address=archive@company.com

# Optional, if the server supports onward REQUIRETLS:
# simplejavamail.locked.defaults.requiretls=true</code></pre>
      <p>Environment variables follow the existing naming convention, for example <code>SIMPLEJAVAMAIL_LOCKED_SMTP_HOST</code>.
        Spring supports the same values through <a href="spring.html#section-spring-locked-configuration">nested YAML or properties</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(
    ConfigLoader.builder().withPropertiesFile(Path.of("mail.properties")).load());

try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder().buildMailer()) {
    Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
        .from("orders@company.com")
        .withRecipients(mail.recipientBuilder().withAddress("customer@example.org").withType(Message.RecipientType.TO).build())
        .withSubject("Your order")
        .withPlainText("Thanks for your order.")
        .buildEmail();
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email); // Also includes archive@company.com.
}

// Fails when building the Mailer, before opening a connection:
mail.mailerBuilder().withSMTPServerHost("another-relay.example.org").buildMailer();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <p>The archive recipient is additive. Your other recipients remain, and an explicit delivery-recipient override still includes the archive.
        Repeated preparation does not keep adding another copy. Ignoring defaults or overrides, replacing a template, or completing an Email early
        does not remove locked settings.</p>
      <p>Conflicts name the locked property and its source without printing either value. The factory's
        <code>getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> shows winning locked entries with their original source and normal secret redaction.
        See <a href="debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">configuration provenance</a> for examples.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>What a lock does, and what it does not do</h3>
    <p class="wide">A lock prevents accidental changes within this factory. It is not a security sandbox: application code can deliberately create
      an independently configured factory or use another transport. It also cannot guarantee final delivery, an arriving DSN, or downstream compliance.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Exact EML keeps its bytes. Compatible envelope additions, such as an archive BCC, are allowed; a lock that would require changing preserved
        content is rejected. Matching readable headers can satisfy a lock without rewriting them. Duplicate Subject, From, or Reply-To fields make a
        lock on that field ambiguous and are rejected; a single Reply-To field can still contain multiple addresses. Existing opaque signatures are
        not treated as proof that a required signing configuration was used.</li>
      <li>Clusters check the selected SMTP configuration as well as the invoking Mailer's restrictions. A different destination cannot silently weaken
        them. Ordinary sends, batches, and open-connection sends all retain the message requirements.</li>
      <li>A caller-owned Session must agree with the applicable locks. If the SMTP username or password is locked, use this factory's
        <code>mailerBuilder()</code> without supplying a Session. Even a matching visible username cannot rule out cached credentials or an
        Authenticator choosing another account. Custom TLS handling is also rejected when it prevents checking the applicable locks.
        Do not mutate a Mailer's Session while it is in use.</li>
      <li><code>CustomMailer</code> receives the governed message, but owns its transport and delivery envelope. Locks on those settings, including archive
        recipients, are rejected with that callback. Content settings that Simple Java Mail prepares still apply.</li>
      <li>Locked DSN requests require explicit provider support and an SMTP server advertising DSN. A third-party adapter cannot silently fall back to
        best-effort behavior. Connection TLS and onward REQUIRETLS remain separate requirements.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

    <section><p class="wide">For more than one approved SMTP destination, see <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-named">named failover configuration</a>.
      Each <code>simplejavamail.mailers.name.*</code> definition resolves independently. A locked relationship protects the captured route, while
      mandatory message locks remain binding through destination changes. Ordinary templates belong to the selected Mailer, including in a cluster.</p></section>
    <section><p class="wide">Optional <a href="/opentelemetry.html#section-privacy">OpenTelemetry endpoint permission</a> uses
      <code>simplejavamail.telemetry.smtp.endpointattributes</code> and its locked counterpart. Activate reporting for Mailers from this factory
      with <code>simplejavamail.telemetry.enabled=true</code>, using the application's initialized global instance or an explicit Java registration.
      Both Boolean settings support locks. The initiating Mailer resolves them independently of the selected SMTP destination.</p></section>
<a id="section-mail-send-observer"></a>
<a id="section-mailer-lifecycle"></a>
<a id="section-batch-and-clustering"></a>
<a id="section-async-queue"></a>
<a id="section-not-reusing-connections"></a>
<a id="section-reusing-connections"></a>
<a id="section-clustering"></a>
<h2>Sending and execution</h2>
<section>
  <p class="wide">Queue limits, send deadlines, cancellation, observers, connection reuse, and Mailer ownership now have their own
    <a href="/sending-and-execution.html">Sending and execution guide</a>. The
    <a href="/smtp-connection-pooling.html">Pool orchestration guide</a> explains which integration owns your Jakarta Mail connections.</p>
</section>

  <section><p class="wide">For safely refused temporary submissions, configure a <a href="/mail-send-retries.html#section-configuration">mail-send retry policy</a>.
    Attempts and delay have no implicit defaults. The selected Mailer owns the schedule; the initiating Mailer owns bounded waiting capacity and replay memory.
    The same property tails work in named definitions, Spring and the <code>simplejavamail.locked.</code> namespace.</p></section>
